# TypeScript & React Coding Standards

## Code Style

- Use **TypeScript strict mode** (enabled in [tsconfig.json](mdc:tsconfig.json))
- Use **functional components** with hooks (no class components)
- Use **arrow functions** for component definitions
- Use **2 spaces** for indentation
- Use **semicolons** consistently
- Prefer **named exports** over default exports for utilities
- Use **PascalCase** for component names
- Use **camelCase** for variables and functions
- Use **kebab-case** for file names

## TypeScript

- Always define **types/interfaces** for props and data structures
- Use **strict null checks** - handle `null` and `undefined` explicitly
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Use **type assertions** sparingly, prefer type guards
- Define **return types** for functions when not obvious

Example:
```typescript
interface CourseProps {
    course: {
        id: number;
        title: string;
        price: number;
        instructor?: {
            name: string;
        };
    };
}

export function CourseCard({ course }: CourseProps): JSX.Element {
    // Implementation
}
```

## React Components

- Use **functional components** with TypeScript
- Use **Inertia.js** for page components: `import { PageProps } from '@inertiajs/react'`
- Extract reusable logic into **custom hooks**
- Keep components **small and focused**
- Use **composition** over inheritance

### Page Components (Inertia)

```typescript
import { PageProps } from '@inertiajs/react';
import { Head } from '@inertiajs/react';

interface Props extends PageProps {
    courses: Array<{
        id: number;
        title: string;
    }>;
}

export default function CoursesIndex({ courses }: Props) {
    return (
        <>
            <Head title="Courses" />
            <div>
                {courses.map(course => (
                    <CourseCard key={course.id} course={course} />
                ))}
            </div>
        </>
    );
}
```

### Reusable Components

```typescript
interface ButtonProps {
    children: React.ReactNode;
    onClick?: () => void;
    variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
    disabled?: boolean;
}

export function Button({ 
    children, 
    onClick, 
    variant = 'primary',
    disabled = false 
}: ButtonProps): JSX.Element {
    return (
        <button
            onClick={onClick}
            disabled={disabled}
            className={`btn btn-${variant}`}
        >
            {children}
        </button>
    );
}
```

## Hooks

- Use **custom hooks** for reusable logic
- Follow naming convention: `use` prefix (e.g., `useTheme`, `useAuth`)
- Extract complex state logic into hooks
- Use **dependency arrays** correctly in `useEffect`

Example:
```typescript
export function useCourses() {
    const [courses, setCourses] = useState<Course[]>([]);
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

    useEffect(() => {
        fetchCourses().then(data => {
            setCourses(data);
            setLoading(false);
        });
    }, []);

    return { courses, loading };
}
```

## Inertia.js Patterns

- Use `router.visit()` for navigation
- Use `router.post()`, `router.put()`, `router.delete()` for form submissions
- Use `useForm` hook from Inertia for form handling
- Use `Head` component for page titles and meta tags

Example:
```typescript
import { useForm } from '@inertiajs/react';

export function CourseForm() {
    const { data, setData, post, processing, errors } = useForm({
        title: '',
        price: 0,
    });

    const handleSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        post(route('courses.store'));
    };

    return (
        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
            <input
                value={data.title}
                onChange={e => setData('title', e.target.value)}
            />
            {errors.title && <div>{errors.title}</div>}
            <button type="submit" disabled={processing}>
                Create Course
            </button>
        </form>
    );
}
```

## File Organization

- **Page components**: `resources/js/pages/{feature}/{page-name}.tsx`
- **Reusable components**: `resources/js/components/{component-name}.tsx`
- **Hooks**: `resources/js/hooks/use-{hook-name}.ts`
- **Types**: `resources/js/types/{feature}.ts`
- **Utils**: `resources/js/utils/{utility-name}.ts`

## Path Aliases

- Use `@/*` alias for imports from `resources/js/`
- Example: `import { Button } from '@/components/button'`
- Example: `import { useAuth } from '@/hooks/use-auth'`

## Styling

- Use **Tailwind CSS** utility classes
- Use **CSS modules** or **styled-components** only when necessary
- Follow **mobile-first** responsive design
- Use **Radix UI** components from `@radix-ui/*` packages
- Use **Lucide React** for icons: `import { Icon } from 'lucide-react'`

Example:
```typescript
<div className="flex flex-col gap-4 p-6 bg-white rounded-lg shadow-md">
    <h2 className="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
        {title}
    </h2>
</div>
```

## State Management

- Use **React hooks** (`useState`, `useReducer`) for local state
- Use **Inertia.js** shared props for server state
- Use **Zustand** or **Context API** only when necessary for global state
- Avoid prop drilling - use context or state management library

## Performance

- Use `React.memo()` for expensive components
- Use `useMemo()` and `useCallback()` appropriately
- Lazy load heavy components: `const Component = lazy(() => import('./Component'))`
- Optimize images and assets

## Error Handling

- Use **error boundaries** for component error handling
- Handle form errors from Inertia: `errors.fieldName`
- Display user-friendly error messages
- Log errors appropriately

## Testing Considerations

- Write **testable components** (pure functions when possible)
- Extract business logic from components
- Use **TypeScript** to catch errors at compile time
- Test user interactions, not implementation details
